A former pasteurizer for Jewel Companies Inc. told a Cook County jury Tuesday that the firm deliberately sold 2% Bluebrook milk under a different label after an outbreak of salmonella poisoning and ordered the cleaning of dairy equipment to hide any trace of the bacteria.
Robert Riley, former pasteurizer at Jewel`s Hillfarm Dairy in Melrose Park, said that for three years before the salmonella outbreak on March 29, 1985 the firm regularly sold 2% Bluebrook milk in Hillfarm 2% milk containers. He said Jewel continued to do that after being notified of the outbreak by state officials and health officials in several counties.
The company`s attorneys acknowledged in opening statements that the 2%
Bluebrook had been identified as the suspected carrier of the disease as early as March 29.
Riley also testified that Hillfarm milk cost 30 cents per gallon more than Bluebrook, meaning that consumers were getting a cheaper milk brand than they were paying for.
Riley, now an assistant plant manager at another Chicago-area firm, said the co-mingling of milk occurred whenever Jewel had a production overrun of one type of milk, meaning that customers also might get the more expensive Hillfarm milk when they paid the lower Bluebrook prices.
Riley told the Circuit Court jury that on March 30, 1985, the day after the first outbreak, orders for the 2% Bluebrook milk were ''way down,'' while Hillfarm orders were ''substantially higher'' than normal.
''That meant that we had to get rid of the Bluebrook milk already produced, so we put it in the Hillfarm containers that week,'' he told the jury in Judge John Breen`s courtroom.
Under questioning from plaintiffs` attorney Lawrence Leck, Riley also said the ''clean-out'' order came on April 8, the day before the dairy was closed. ''We had heard on the radio about another outbreak and there was speculation that the dairy was going to close,'' Riley said.
''I asked Guenther Lindholm (head of sanitation at the dairy) if he still wanted me to clean out all of the lines. He went away for a few minutes, then came back and said to clean everything out.''
Leck questioned Riley about the ''clean-out'' order in order to show the jury that Jewel told its employees to clean out dairy equipment to prevent state inspectors from taking milk samples that would show the presence of salmonella.
Jewel is expected to begin presenting its side of the case in January. The firm`s attorneys, Richard Phelan and Richard Devine, have contended that Jewel employees acted as reasonably as can be expected based on the limited information they had available before April 9.
They also have argued that the March 29 outbreak was limited to 2%
Bluebrook milk, that complaints dropped daily until the second outbreak and that the cause of the outbreak remains unknown despite a 5-month task force investigation.
Earlier Tuesday, a second Lake County health department employee told the jury of being threatened with a lawsuit by Jewel if he told the public about the March 29 outbreak.
Ted Byers, assistant director of environmental health for Lake County, said he was threatened by Michael McClory, former head of quality assurance at the dairy, after Byers called McClory to ask him if he would ''pull the milk'' from production after Lake County officials had received more than 10 reports of salmonella that day which they had linked to Jewel milk.
''McClory said, `No,` when I asked him to pull the milk,'' Byers testified under questioning from another plaintiffs` attorney, William Harte. ''McClory was looking at the numbers. There were 30 to 40 complaints scattered around the Chicago area and the dairy was processing 200,000 gallons of milk a day.
''He felt those were not big numbers,'' Byers said. ''He knew what was going on because he had talked that day with state health officials, who had gotten reports from other counties. He knew there were discussions going on among state employees about asking Jewel to pull the milk.
''He said if we did anything there would be litigation,'' Byers testified. ''We could have gone to court or warned the public on our own, but we didn`t. It was not my decision.''
Outside court, Byers said the decision not publicize the link to Jewel milk that day was made by his superior, Dr. Steven Potsic, executive director of the Lake County Health Department.
Potsic first told the jury of the threat to Byers by McClory when he testified in the trial on Nov. 19.
