Mr Prendiville, nephew of Cottesloe Beach Hotel owner Peter Prendiville, said the "guilt thing has been done" and instead wanted to highlight the work done by volunteers.
"We didn't want to just show dogs or cats throughout all the galleries," he said.
"We want to make sure the focus is on the people who keep the shelters going and work for nothing."
"There's always going to be an element of sadness about these shelters, but it's been done quite a lot where people are taken down that sad path and it was good to meet these people and see there are quite surprisingly good stories."
Four photographers took the project on, each photographing a shelter – The Dogs' Refuge Home and Cat Haven, both in Shenton Park, K9 Dog Rescue in Nambeelup and Poundwatch in Banjup.
Proceeds will be split evenly between the four shelters.
A lot of people are under the impression there's something wrong with the dogs in there.
The photos are also meant to do away with preconceived notions about shelter animals.
"A lot of people are under the impression there's something wrong with the dogs in there," Mr Prendiville said.
"But there are a lot of circumstances where the owner no longer has the means to take care of the dog or they didn't realise how much love and attention they required in the first place."
He said some of the stories were heartbreaking.
"When we took photos at the Shenton Park shelter, a dog in one of the photos - the older woman sitting with all the towels and laundry - that dog had been rescued from its owners lap," he said.
"It had been sitting on its owners lap since its owner died."
He called Poundwatch founder Sue Down, who lives on her Banjup property with around 50 animals, as a "remarkable" person.
Ms Down has been rescuing dogs that would otherwise be put down for almost 40 years.
