Most novels are narrated in the past tense, some (including Le Carré's most recent, Absolute Friends) in the present tense, but a few, like The Constant Gardener, oscillate between the two. In this novel, the effects of the shifts from past to present tense and back again are counter-intuitive. The past tense is for action, the present tense is for memory.
For 50 pages or so the novel is narrated in the past tense, from the point of view of the British diplomat Sandy Woodrow. We follow him to the Nairobi house of a murdered British woman, Tessa Quayle, in the company of her shocked husband. Ostensibly Woodrow is to help Quayle recover personal documents. In fact, he wants to find and destroy a love letter that he once drunkenly wrote to Tessa. Back in the Quayles' house, the past floods back.
"He reached the middle of the room and stopped, arrested by the power of memory." We hardly need to be told about this "power", for the narrative now switches to the present tense, re-performing Woodrow's last meeting with the woman who is now dead. " 'I thought the best thing I could do was call by,' he begins sternly." Now he is in the same room at a different time, and Tessa is smiling at his poor excuse for his visit. "Woodrow can't see the smile because she is backlit. But he can hear it in her voice." It seems to be happening now.
This is the first of many occasions on which the present tense is used to show that something is taking place in a character's head, brought to immediacy by the force of memory. For most of the novel, the memories are Quayle's, retrieving the personality of his wife, but also bringing back the events that will enable him to work out why she was killed.
He is first taken from past to present tense as he leaves Nairobi airport after her murder and sees a poster advertising the miraculous drugs of the sinister Three Bees company. "The poster held him. Exactly as it had held Tessa. Staring rigidly up at it, Justin is listening to her joyous protestations at his right side." He is remembering the couple's first arrival in Kenya, when they saw the very same poster. He is also sensing a clue to Tessa's fate. From then on, such tense shifts structure the narrative. When a paragraph begins, say, "It is evening...", you know that you are being taken into Quayle's memories.
